{
	title: 'A <question> tag marks a turn that waits for the user',
	spec: 'A reply asking the user ends with <question>one line</question> instead of <summary>. Both clients show question content inline at its original position, without XML syntax, including transcript replay; summary content remains notification metadata unless it is the only answer. Incomplete notification tags stay hidden during streaming; literal tags in Markdown code remain literal. The question supplies an attention notification and a persistent tab attention mark. Invariant: a question is visible and automatic agent activity cannot substitute for a human reply. After an asking turn, queued messages and incoming agent messages (including steering and subagent reports) remain durably queued and visible until the human sends a normal reply; queued work runs after a later final answer. Human steering and explicit /queue next remain overrides. Slash commands keep their existing command behavior. Waiting derives from history, with no new state store, tool, or question UI. SYSTEM.md explains that the question is displayed and agent messages wait. Both clients share the behavior. Raised in sessions 166-gig and 165-gab on 2026-10-06 and refined in 167-fox on 2026-10-07; a separate tag avoids unreliable prose classification and summary attributes.',
